      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the sum of all the positive divisors of 91, we first need to determine its prime factorization. We start by checking for divisibility by the smallest prime numbers.\n\n1. **Check for divisibility by 2:**\n   - 91 is odd, so it is not divisible by 2.\n\n2. **Check for divisibility by 3:**\n   - Sum of the digits of 91 is \\(9 + 1 = 10\\), which is not divisible by 3.\n   - Therefore, 91 is not divisible by 3.\n\n3. **Check for divisibility by 5:**\n   - 91 does not end in 0 or 5, so it is not divisible by 5.\n\n4. **Check for divisibility by 7:**\n   - Perform the division: \\(91 \\div 7 = 13\\).\n   - Since 91 divided by 7 equals 13, which is an integer, 91 is divisible by 7.\n   - Therefore, \\(91 = 7 \\times 13\\).\n\nNext, we verify that 13 is a prime number by checking for divisibility by prime numbers less than its square root (approximately 3.61).\n- 13 is not divisible by 2, 3, or 5.\n\nSince 13 is a prime number, the prime factorization of 91 is:\n\\[ 91 = 7 \\times 13 \\]\n\nTo find the sum of all the positive divisors, we use the formula for the sum of divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\):\n\\[ \\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k}) \\]\n\nFor \\( 91 = 7^1 \\times 13^1 \\):\n\\[ \\sigma(91) = (1 + 7)(1 + 13) \\]\n\\[ \\sigma(91) = 8 \\times 14 \\]\n\\[ \\sigma(91) = 112 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all the positive divisors of 91 is \\(\\boxed{112}\\).",
