    "text": "To find the sum of all the positive divisors of 91, we first need to determine its prime factorization. We can do this by dividing 91 by the smallest prime number, which is 2. Since 91 is not divisible by 2, we move on to the next prime number, which is 3. Again, 91 is not divisible by 3. We continue this process until we find a prime number that divides 91. In this case, 91 is divisible by 7, so we have:\n\n91 = 7 × 13\n\nNow that we have the prime factorization of 91, we can use it to find the sum of its divisors. The formula for the sum of divisors of a number \\( n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k} \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\n\nFor 91, we have \\( n = 7^1 \\times 13^1 \\). So, the sum of the divisors is:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(91) = (1 + 7)(1 + 13) = 8 \\times 14 = 112\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all the positive divisors of 91 is \\(\\boxed{112}\\).",
